# Rank 1233 / CVE-2025-5115 jetty/jetty.project: an HTTP/2 peer can make the
# server generate RST_STREAM frames for it, and nothing charges those frames
# against the connection's rate control (CWE-400, CWE-770, the weakness the
# "MadeYouReset" family of HTTP/2 advisories describes). Jetty meters the
# frames it receives -- RateControl.onEvent returns false once a connection has
# spent its budget, and the parser closes the connection with
# ENHANCE_YOUR_CALM -- but HTTP2Session.reset() wrote the reset frame through
# control(), closed the stream and dropped it from the session registry with no
# charge at all. A peer that keeps sending frames the server must answer with a
# stream error (a zero-delta WINDOW_UPDATE, a flow-control overflow, a frame on
# a closed stream) therefore drives stream creation, stream teardown and
# outbound frame emission at whatever rate it can write, and each teardown
# frees a slot in the concurrent-stream limit immediately, so the limit stops
# bounding the work in flight.
#
# Repository names: rank 1232 scans eclipse/jetty.project and this rank scans
# jetty/jetty.project. Both names resolve to the same repository -- the GitHub
# API answers repos/eclipse/jetty.project with full_name jetty/jetty.project
# and fork false -- so the project moved organisation and both pool rows are
# correct as written. The two CVEs are genuinely different: CVE-2019-17638 is a
# 9.4 buffer release defect and CVE-2025-5115 is this 12.0 rate-control gap.
# No pool correction is needed for either row.
#
# Pool commit f9ee390478 carries no flag; read its diff per Step 0's note that
# flags are not the only source of pool errors, and confirmed it is the real
# fix: it is the merge of pull request 13449, whose GitHub title is "HTTP2Session
# cleanups to address CVE-2025-5115", and its combined diff touches only the
# HTTP/2 module -- HTTP2Session.java, the four parser classes that expose
# RateControl, the module configuration that lowers the default event budget,
# and two tests. Its first parent 6b1a663d is the vulnerable state. The same
# change went to the 9.4 and 10.0 branches as pull requests 13461 and 13462.
#
# FIXED. Nothing labelled the teardown path: a bounded scan of the whole
# jetty-core tree at the vulnerable parent reports 1263 findings and none of
# them names HTTP2Session.reset.
# Added code.StreamResetWithoutRateControl (VYQL-DOS-044) with a Java binding
# at function scope. The binding asks for three things together, because the
# weakness is the conjunction and not any one of them: the method receives the
# reset frame or the stream error that describes it (ResetFrame,
# RstStreamFrame, Http2ResetFrame, StreamException -- the frame types the Java
# HTTP/2 stacks use), it writes that frame out itself (Jetty's control(), or
# writeRstStream / sendRstStream / writeStreamReset / sendStreamReset /
# writeReset / sendReset in the other stacks), and it removes the stream from
# the session registry. Any rate-control name on that path suppresses it, in
# both spellings applications use: the interface Jetty documents
# (rateControlOnEvent, getRateControl, rateControl.onEvent, onEvent) and an
# ordinary token bucket (rateLimiter.tryAcquire, limiter.acquire, throttle,
# checkRate), plus a rateControl or rateLimiter field name and the
# ENHANCE_YOUR_CALM error the charge produces when it fails.
#
# All three conjuncts are needed, and the rejecting tests below are the reason.
# The third test is the same teardown guarded by a token bucket under a name
# Jetty never uses, so the binding cannot be keyed to one library's limiter.
# The fourth is HTTP2Stream.reset() copied from the same revision: it receives
# the frame and it removes the stream, but it forwards the write to
# session.reset() rather than performing it, so the charge belongs to the method
# it forwards to and flagging the caller would report the same defect twice.
#
# Measured on the real checkout, bounded (timeout 180, --max-ram 4GB,
# --cache off). Scanning jetty-core/jetty-http2 (173 java files) at the
# vulnerable parent 6b1a663d reports 98 findings, one of them VYQL-DOS-044 at
# HTTP2Session.java:816, the reset() method the fix changes; the same module at
# f9ee390478 reports 97, the same set minus that finding. Widening to the whole
# jetty-core tree (2197 java files, which holds the HTTP/2 client, the HTTP/2
# server, the HTTP/3 session that has the same reset shape, and their tests)
# still reports VYQL-DOS-044 exactly once at the vulnerable parent and zero
# times at the fix, so the three conjuncts do not fire on the neighbouring
# session implementations. The whole repository exceeds the 180s bound, so
# jetty-core is the real-checkout evidence. False positives: the same binding
# over an unrelated Java corpus already cloned for this corpus,
# jackson-databind/src (721 java files), reports VYQL-DOS-044 zero times, and
# the full spec suite (4376 specs) passes unchanged.
#

test "rank1233: the session sends RST_STREAM and tears the stream down with no rate charge"
  lang java
  file HTTP2Session.java
  expect VYQL-DOS-044
  expect_review code.StreamResetWithoutRateControl
  code
  ```
  package org.eclipse.jetty.http2;

  import org.eclipse.jetty.http2.frames.ResetFrame;
  import org.eclipse.jetty.util.Callback;

  public abstract class HTTP2Session extends AbstractLifeCycle implements Session, Parser.Listener
  {
      void reset(HTTP2Stream stream, ResetFrame frame, Callback callback)
      {
          control(stream, Callback.from(() ->
          {
              if (stream != null)
              {
                  stream.close();
                  removeStream(stream);
              }
          }, callback), frame);
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1233: the teardown path charges the connection rate control before writing the frame"
  lang java
  file HTTP2Session.java
  reject VYQL-DOS-044
  reject_review code.StreamResetWithoutRateControl
  code
  ```
  package org.eclipse.jetty.http2;

  import org.eclipse.jetty.http2.frames.ResetFrame;
  import org.eclipse.jetty.util.Callback;

  public abstract class HTTP2Session extends AbstractLifeCycle implements Session, Parser.Listener
  {
      void reset(HTTP2Stream stream, ResetFrame frame, Callback callback)
      {
          if (rateControlOnEvent(frame))
          {
              control(stream, Callback.from(() ->
              {
                  if (stream != null)
                  {
                      stream.close();
                      removeStream(stream);
                  }
              }, callback), frame);
          }
          else
          {
              onConnectionFailure(ErrorCode.ENHANCE_YOUR_CALM_ERROR.code, "invalid_rst_stream_frame_rate");
          }
      }

      private boolean rateControlOnEvent(Object event)
      {
          return getParser().rateControlOnEvent(event);
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1233: a teardown path guarded by a token-bucket limiter under a different name"
  lang java
  file Http2ConnectionHandler.java
  reject VYQL-DOS-044
  reject_review code.StreamResetWithoutRateControl
  code
  ```
  package com.example.http2;

  import com.example.http2.frames.ResetFrame;

  public class Http2ConnectionHandler
  {
      private final RateLimiter rateLimiter;

      void resetStream(Http2Stream stream, ResetFrame frame, Callback callback)
      {
          if (!rateLimiter.tryAcquire())
          {
              connectionError(Http2Error.ENHANCE_YOUR_CALM, "reset_rate_exceeded");
              return;
          }
          writeRstStream(stream, frame, callback);
          stream.close();
          removeStream(stream);
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1233: the stream-level reset entry point delegates the frame write to the session"
  lang java
  file HTTP2Stream.java
  reject VYQL-DOS-044
  reject_review code.StreamResetWithoutRateControl
  code
  ```
  package org.eclipse.jetty.http2;

  import org.eclipse.jetty.http2.frames.ResetFrame;
  import org.eclipse.jetty.util.Callback;

  public class HTTP2Stream implements Stream, Attachable, Closeable, Callback, Dumpable
  {
      @Override
      public void reset(ResetFrame frame, Callback callback)
      {
          if (LOG.isDebugEnabled())
              LOG.debug("Resetting {} {}", frame, this);

          int flowControlLength;
          boolean reset = true;
          Throwable resetFailure = null;
          try (AutoLock ignored = lock.lock())
          {
              flowControlLength = drain();
              if (localReset)
              {
                  reset = false;
                  resetFailure = failure;
              }
              else
              {
                  localReset = true;
                  failure = new EOFException("reset");
              }
          }

          session.dataConsumed(this, flowControlLength);

          if (reset)
          {
              session.reset(this, frame, callback);
          }
          else
          {
              close();
              session.removeStream(this);
              if (resetFailure == null)
                  callback.succeeded();
              else
                  callback.failed(resetFailure);
          }
      }
  }
  ```
